---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { techArticle, dataset } from '../../../lib/schema';
import pagesData from '../../../data/pages.json';
import report from '../../../data/geo-report.generated.json';
import EntityNote from '../../../components/EntityNote.astro';

const route = '/en/reports/model-access-benchmark';
const page = pagesData.pages.find((p) => p.route === route)!;
const url = pagesData.site + route;
const published = report.published === true;
// Publishing needs both gates open: real data (published) + draft removed (editorial sign-off). Either closed => noindex.
const indexable = published && !page.draft;
const latencyAvailable = report.latencyAvailable === true;
// Fail-closed wording: with no window_days we must NOT fall back to "30-day rolling window" —
// that would assert an unmeasured window. Same for the column header and any latency promise.
const windowLabel = report.window_days ? `${report.window_days}-day rolling window` : 'current probe batch (rolling-window statistics not yet enabled)';
const availHeader = report.window_days ? `${report.window_days}-day availability` : 'Availability';
const scopeText = latencyAvailable ? 'availability plus P50/P95 latency' : 'availability';
const fmt = (n: number | null) => (n === null || n === undefined ? '—' : (Number.isInteger(n) ? String(n) : n.toFixed(1)));
---

<Base route={route} theme="dark" noindex={!indexable}>
  <Fragment slot="jsonld">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(techArticle({ headline: 'smaapi Overseas Model Access Index', description: page.description, url, inLanguage: 'en', datePublished: page.datePublished, dateModified: page.dateModified }))} />
    {published && (
      <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(dataset({
        name: 'smaapi Overseas Model Access Index',
        description: page.description,
        url,
        inLanguage: 'en',
        temporalCoverage: report.as_of ? `${report.as_of} (${windowLabel})` : windowLabel,
        measurementTechnique: 'Active probing and production monitoring by the SMA gateway; injected at build time, never hand-filled.',
        datePublished: page.datePublished,
        dateModified: page.dateModified,
        variableMeasured: latencyAvailable
          ? [availHeader, 'P50 latency (ms)', 'P95 latency (ms)', 'Sample count']
          : [availHeader, 'Sample count'],
      }))} />
    )}
  </Fragment>

  <h1>smaapi Overseas Model Access Index: Measured Access of Connected Models</h1>
  <p class="answer">
    The smaapi Overseas Model Access Index is a measured record of the overseas LLMs x access channels the SMA gateway (www.smaapi.com) is actually connected to: it reports {scopeText} and sample count per model, obtained by active probing rather than vendor claims. Only models above the publish threshold are listed; figures are injected by the build pipeline and never hand-filled; unconnected or under-sampled models do not appear.
  </p>

  {!published && (
    <div class="answer" style="border-left-color:#f2c75c">
      <strong>No figures published this period.</strong> This page follows a strict "no real data, no publication" rule: until the data interface (the SMA gateway <code>/internal/metrics/geo-export</code> endpoint) is wired and the first real measurements land, only methodology is shown — never an unmeasured number. The statistical definitions and structure of the index follow below.
    </div>
  )}

  <h2>1. How are these numbers measured?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Data source</strong>: active probing and production monitoring by the SMA gateway; injected by the build pipeline, never hand-filled.</li>
    <li><strong>Window</strong>: {windowLabel}{report.as_of ? `, as of ${report.as_of}` : ''}.</li>
    <li><strong>P95 definition</strong>: {report.p95_definition}</li>
    <li><strong>Publish threshold</strong>: a model x channel with a sample count below {report.thresholdSampleCount} is excluded from the main table (under-sampled figures are not publishable).</li>
    <li><strong>How it's built</strong>: generated by the build pipeline; every number traces back to a row in the gateway export.</li>
    <li><strong>Machine-readable</strong>: the same data is published as a <a href="https://schema.org/Dataset" rel="nofollow">schema.org Dataset</a> (defined there as "A body of structured information describing some topic(s) of interest.") and served at a <a href="/data/model-access-index.json">JSON endpoint</a>, so the definitions travel with the numbers when they are quoted elsewhere.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Which models are connected, and what did they measure?</h2>
  {published ? (
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Model</th><th>Channel</th><th>{availHeader}</th><th>P50 (ms)</th><th>P95 (ms)</th><th>Samples</th><th>As of</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {report.rows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.model_alias}</td>
            <td>{r.channel_type}</td>
            <td>{fmt(r.availability_pct)}%</td>
            <td>{fmt(r.p50_ms)}</td>
            <td>{fmt(r.p95_ms)}</td>
            <td>{r.sample_count}</td>
            <td>{r.as_of}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  ) : (
    <p>Once the data interface is wired, this section lists, row by row, each currently connected model x channel whose sample count meets the threshold — model x channel x 30-day availability x P50/P95 latency x sample count. Unconnected or under-sampled models do not appear.</p>
  )}
  {published && !latencyAvailable && (
    <>
      <p class="footnote">This period is the "availability-only" edition: latency percentiles (P50/P95) will ship once the gateway records per-request latency, so the latency columns are left blank (missing, not filled). Availability comes from active probing; see methodology above.</p>
      <p>The columns are left blank rather than filled with an average, because an average flattens exactly the tail that enterprise traffic runs into. As Google's SRE team puts it in the chapter on Service Level Objectives:</p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>"Monitoring and alerting based only on the average latency would show no change in behavior over the course of the day, when there are in fact significant changes in the tail latency […]"</p>
        <cite>Source: <a href="https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/" rel="nofollow">Google SRE Book · Service Level Objectives</a> (bracketed ellipsis ours)</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p>So the latency column either carries P50/P95 percentiles or stays empty — no mean value standing in for them.</p>
    </>
  )}
  {published && report.droppedBelowThreshold > 0 && (
    <p class="footnote">{report.droppedBelowThreshold} model x channel pair(s) were excluded this period for not meeting the sample threshold ({report.thresholdSampleCount}) — recorded honestly, not padded with thin samples.</p>
  )}

  <h2>3. Can this availability be read as an SLA?</h2>
  <p>
    No — the two are measured differently. A cloud provider's SLA is a contractual, creditable commitment. Amazon Bedrock's SLA, for instance, defines availability like this:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"'Availability' is calculated for each 5-minute interval as the percentage of Requests processed by Amazon Bedrock that do not fail with Errors and relate solely to the Amazon Bedrock APIs for models that are available on Amazon Bedrock."</p>
    <p>"'Monthly Uptime Percentage' for a given AWS region is calculated as the average of the Availability for all 5-minute intervals in a monthly billing cycle."</p>
    <cite>Source: <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/sla/" rel="nofollow">Amazon Bedrock Service Level Agreement</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Three differences from this page: <strong>unit of measurement</strong> — an SLA aggregates real production requests over 5-minute intervals, this page counts probe rounds; <strong>scope</strong> — an SLA is computed per AWS region over a monthly billing cycle and excludes the SLA Exclusions it lists, this page is per model x channel over the {windowLabel}; <strong>force</strong> — an SLA is a claimable term, this page is a measurement record. The figures here tell you whether a route is currently reachable and steady; they are not an availability commitment. Contractual availability terms are per enterprise contract.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Why are cost figures not published yet?</h2>
  <p>
    The first version does not show cross-channel cost comparisons. These will ship as a separate version only once real billing data, currency, FX date, token accounting, and sample thresholds are all fixed. Left blank here rather than filled with estimates.
  </p>

  <h2>5. How are removed models recorded?</h2>
  <p>
    Each monthly release records the models added or removed and why. The record of a model removed for failing probes is itself evidence of the index's honesty — removals are written down, not quietly dropped.
  </p>

  <p class="footnote">
    Trademarks: OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and their model names belong to their respective holders. SMA (www.smaapi.com) has no official partnership with these trademark holders; model names are used nominatively. Amazon Web Services and Amazon Bedrock are trademarks of Amazon.com, Inc. or its affiliates; this page cites their public documentation nominatively. Availability and latency are measured statistics and do not constitute an SLA; SLA terms are per enterprise contract.
  </p>

  <EntityNote lang="en" />


  <p class="related">
    Related: <a href="/en/overseas-llm-high-availability">High-availability access to overseas LLMs</a> ·
    <a href="/en/compliant-overseas-llm-access">Compliant access to overseas LLMs</a> ·
    <a href="/en/what-is-llm-gateway">What is an enterprise LLM gateway</a>
  </p>
</Base>
